⚡ 8️⃣ Volatile Keyword

Ensures visibility of changes across threads.

Does not ensure atomicity.

volatile boolean flag = false;

Thread t = new Thread(() -> {
    while(!flag) {}
    System.out.println("Flag changed!");
});
t.start();

Thread.sleep(1000);
flag = true;


Without volatile, thread t may never see the change to flag (cached value).

🧰 9️⃣ Executor Framework

Instead of manually managing threads, Java provides executors.

Example
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class ExecutorExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);

        executor.submit(() -> System.out.println("Task 1 executed"));
        executor.submit(() -> System.out.println("Task 2 executed"));

        executor.shutdown();
    }
}


Why use Executors?
✅ Manage thread pools efficiently
✅ Avoid thread explosion
✅ Reuse threads
✅ Simplifies concurrency management

🧮 1️⃣0️⃣ Callable and Future

If you want a thread to return a value, use Callable.

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class CallableExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();

        Callable<Integer> task = () -> {
            Thread.sleep(1000);
            return 42;
        };

        Future<Integer> result = executor.submit(task);
        System.out.println("Result = " + result.get());

        executor.shutdown();
    }
}

🧠 1️⃣1️⃣ Thread Communication

Threads communicate using wait(), notify(), and notifyAll() (used in producer-consumer problems).

Example (simple)
class SharedResource {
    synchronized void produce() throws InterruptedException {
        System.out.println("Producing...");
        wait(); // release lock
        System.out.println("Resumed after consuming");
    }

    synchronized void consume() throws InterruptedException {
        Thread.sleep(1000);
        System.out.println("Consuming...");
        notify(); // wake producer
    }
}

