1️⃣ What is the difference between synchronized and Lock?
Feature	synchronized	Lock (from java.util.concurrent.locks)
Type	Keyword (built-in)	Interface-based (explicit control)
Scope	Block or method	Any code block
Reentrancy	Reentrant	ReentrantLock is reentrant
Try Lock	Not possible	tryLock() allows timeout / non-blocking
Fairness	Not configurable	Can create fair locks
Condition support	Not available	Condition objects allow fine-grained waits
Release	Auto released	Must release manually via unlock()
🧩 Example:
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class LockExample {
    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    private int counter = 0;

    public void increment() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            counter++;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }
}


💡 Tip: Lock is preferred when you need:

Fine-grained control

Try-lock mechanism

Timeout or multiple condition variables

2️⃣ Difference between volatile and synchronized
Aspect	volatile	synchronized
Purpose	Visibility	Atomicity + Visibility
Atomicity	❌ No	✅ Yes
Locking	No lock	Implicit lock
Use case	Status flags, configuration updates	Critical sections
Example:
volatile boolean flag = false;


This ensures visibility — all threads will see the latest value of flag.
But if multiple threads modify it (e.g., increment counter), it’s not atomic → use synchronization or atomic variables.

3️⃣ What is a volatile variable actually doing in JVM?

Tells the JVM and CPU not to cache the variable.

Every read/write happens directly from main memory.

Provides happens-before guarantee — write by one thread is visible to another.

4️⃣ How does ConcurrentHashMap work internally?

Before Java 8:

Divided into segments (each segment = smaller lock-protected hash table).

Multiple threads could update different segments concurrently.

After Java 8:

Uses CAS (Compare-And-Swap) for thread-safe operations.

No segment locks.

Buckets are handled using synchronized blocks and Node arrays.

On high contention, uses tree bins (red-black tree) instead of linked lists.

💡 Example:

ConcurrentHashMap<String, Integer> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
map.put("A", 1);
map.put("B", 2);
System.out.println(map.get("A"));


✅ Thread-safe without locking the entire map.
✅ No ConcurrentModificationException.

5️⃣ What are Atomic variables in Java?

Classes like:

AtomicInteger

AtomicLong

AtomicBoolean

AtomicReference

use CAS (Compare-And-Swap) internally for atomic operations without synchronization.

Example:
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class AtomicExample {
    private static AtomicInteger count = new AtomicInteger(0);

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Runnable task = () -> {
            for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) count.incrementAndGet();
        };

        Thread t1 = new Thread(task);
        Thread t2 = new Thread(task);
        t1.start(); t2.start();
        t1.join(); t2.join();

        System.out.println(count.get()); // Always 2000
    }
}


✅ No race condition
✅ No lock overhead
✅ Non-blocking performance

6️⃣ What is ThreadLocal and why is it used?

ThreadLocal provides thread-specific variables.
Each thread has its own copy of the variable.

Example:
public class ThreadLocalExample {
    private static ThreadLocal<Integer> threadId = ThreadLocal.withInitial(() -> 0);

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Runnable task = () -> {
            threadId.set((int) (Math.random() * 100));
            System.out.println(Thread.currentThread().getName() + " → " + threadId.get());
        };

        new Thread(task).start();
        new Thread(task).start();
    }
}


✅ Each thread gets its own isolated data.
💡 Used in: database connections, user session data, request context in web apps.

7️⃣ Difference between wait(), sleep(), and join()
Method	Belongs To	Releases Lock	Purpose
wait()	Object class	✅ Yes	Wait until notify() is called
sleep()	Thread class	❌ No	Pause thread for given time
join()	Thread class	❌ No	Wait until another thread finishes
Example:
Thread t = new Thread(() -> {
    try { Thread.sleep(2000); } catch (InterruptedException e) {}
    System.out.println("Thread done!");
});

t.start();
t.join(); // main waits for t
System.out.println("Main continues...");

8️⃣ What is Deadlock?

When two or more threads wait indefinitely for each other’s lock.

Example:
class A {
    synchronized void methodA(B b) {
        System.out.println("Thread 1 locked A");
        try { Thread.sleep(100); } catch (InterruptedException e) {}
        b.last();
    }
    synchronized void last() { System.out.println("Inside A.last()"); }
}

class B {
    synchronized void methodB(A a) {
        System.out.println("Thread 2 locked B");
        try { Thread.sleep(100); } catch (InterruptedException e) {}
        a.last();
    }
    synchronized void last() { System.out.println("Inside B.last()"); }
}

public class DeadlockExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        A a = new A();
        B b = new B();

        new Thread(() -> a.methodA(b)).start();
        new Thread(() -> b.methodB(a)).start();
    }
}


💣 Both threads hold one lock and wait for the other → Deadlock.

Avoid by:

Lock ordering

Timeout-based locking (tryLock(timeout))

9️⃣ What is a Daemon Thread?

Background thread that runs in support of user threads.

JVM exits when only daemon threads are left.

Example:
Thread t = new Thread(() -> {
    while (true) System.out.println("Daemon thread running...");
});
t.setDaemon(true);
t.start();


💡 Used for: Garbage Collector, background cleanup, etc.

🔟 What is the difference between ExecutorService and ForkJoinPool?
Feature	ExecutorService	ForkJoinPool
Task Type	Independent	Recursive (divide & conquer)
Task Interface	Runnable / Callable	RecursiveTask / RecursiveAction
Use Case	Thread pool management	Parallel stream / divide tasks
Example — ForkJoin
class SumTask extends RecursiveTask<Integer> {
    int start, end;
    SumTask(int start, int end) { this.start = start; this.end = end; }

    protected Integer compute() {
        if (end - start <= 5) {
            int sum = 0;
            for (int i = start; i <= end; i++) sum += i;
            return sum;
        } else {
            int mid = (start + end) / 2;
            SumTask left = new SumTask(start, mid);
            SumTask right = new SumTask(mid + 1, end);
            left.fork();
            return right.compute() + left.join();
        }
    }
}

